// The first procedure here that reads a geometry it is not running over.
//
// **`pairs` says this L2 takes two geometries and produces one.** `L2 : Geometry
// -> Geometry` is an endomorphism, and the two ways to break it are now both
// spelled: `amplify` breaks it on the *count* axis, one element in and several
// out, and this breaks it on the *arity*.
//
//   karakuri-cli --set lattice_shell.kir,sphere_shell.kir,morph.kir,soft_points.kir
//
// **The correspondence is the slot index**, so element 5 of one geometry becomes
// element 5 of the other. That is only the same element in both while nothing
// moves one, which is why both sources must be static — no `spawn`, no `kill()`
// — and why the Set refuses the pair rather than matching each element with a
// stranger.
//
// **The paired geometry is never drawn on its own.** A pairing Set is one source
// made of two simulations, with one chain and one set of renderers over it, so
// "is the far end also on screen?" is a question with no place to be asked.
//
// One `consumes` covers both sides: pairing reads the same attribute from each,
// so declaring it once is declaring it for both.

proc morph {
  kind  L2
  pairs

  // 0 is the near geometry and 1 is the paired one. A fader, a signal binding,
  // a transition and a published control all reach it, because it is an
  // ordinary `param` — the same argument `weight` makes one layer down.
  param k : float [0.0, 1.0] = 0.0

  consumes position, tint

  deform {
    // `mix` here is componentwise, so the blend factor is widened rather than
    // passed as a scalar: this language's `mix` takes three of one type.
    let w = vec3(k, k, k);

    position = mix(position, other.position, w);
    tint     = mix(tint, other.tint, w);
  }
}
